{"abstract":"This extended abstract explores the ‘limits’ of legally permitted gameplay and game production in relation to gambling-like products within video games: specifically, how the Belgian ‘ban’ on paid loot boxes has affected players’ gameplay experience.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Xiao, Leon Y.","Denoo, Maarten"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-677297"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Belgium"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","KU Leuven"],"keyword":["Gambling Mechanics","Game Studies","Legal Regulations","Loot boxes","Player Experience"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"what did players think about belgium s ban on loot boxes","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dca","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.31219/osf.io/3f2zj","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-677297","ids":["itu-05c86c8c-6931-4ae2-8645-e61baef9fdd3"],"keyword":["Gambling Mechanics","Game Studies","Legal Regulations","Loot Boxes","Player Experience"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-2-16B4F694BC4EE1CD9D0C3A60CF681EF8"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.31219/osf.io/3f2zj","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"rem","url":"https://osf.io/3f2zj/download","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Digital Play","IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design","IT University of Copenhagen, Play, Culture, and AI, Games Research Group"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"be","key":"ku leuven","loc":["Belgium","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"KU Leuven","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Leon Y.","key":"xiaopau","last":"Xiao","locid":"0a2a2206-180b-4443-bdb7-92deba889221","name":"Xiao, Leon Y.","orcid":"0000-0003-0709-0777","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Maarten","key":"denoopau","last":"Denoo","name":"Denoo, Maarten","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.31219/osf.io/3f2zj","pages":"1-4","place":"Sevilla, España","publisher":"Digital Games Research Association","status":"p","subtitle":"Limits and Margins of Games","title":"Digital Games Research Association (DiGRA) International Conference 2023"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-05c86c8c-6931-4ae2-8645-e61baef9fdd3"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"What did players think about Belgium’s ‘ban’ on loot boxes?","type":"Conference Abstract","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-677297","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"Loot boxes are purchased in video games to obtain randomised rewards of varying value and are thus psychologically akin to gambling. Disclosing the probabilities of obtaining loot box rewards may reduce overspending, in a similar vein to related disclosure approaches in gambling. Presently, this consumer protection measure has been adopted as law only in the People’s Republic of China (PRC). In other countries, the videogaming industry has generally adopted this measure as self-regulation. However, self-regulation conflicts with commercial interests and might not maximally promote public welfare. The loot box prevalence rate amongst the 100 highest-grossing UK iPhone games was 77% in mid-2021. The compliance rate with probability disclosure industry self-regulation was only 64.0%, significantly lower than that of PRC legal regulation (95.6%). In addition, UK games generally made insufficiently prominent and difficult-to-access disclosures both in-game and on the game’s official website. Significantly fewer UK games disclosed probabilities on their official websites (21.3%) when compared to 72.5% of PRC games. Only one of 75 UK games (1.3%) adopted the most prominent disclosure format of automatically displaying the probabilities on the in-game purchase page. Policymakers should demand more accountable forms of industry self-regulation or impose direct legal regulation to ensure consumer protection.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Xiao, Leon Y.","Henderson, Laura L.","Newall, Philip W.S."],"cuid":["S01-000.00-718911"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","OECD","Oceania"],"country":["Australia","United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","CQUniversity","The Honourable Society of Lincoln's Inn","University of Bristol"],"keyword":["Consumer protection","Loot boxes","Probability Disclosure","Self Regulation vs Legal Regulation","Videogame Industry"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"what are the odds poor compliance with uk loot box probability disclosure industry self regulation","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0286681","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-718911","ids":["itu-be07b61b-36da-449d-bad5-fa04f50b95c1"],"keyword":["Consumer Protection","Loot Boxes","Probability Disclosure","Self-Regulation vs. Legal Regulation","Videogame Industry"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-B5434696B43FE8DD8B663F1BC5977B67"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0286681","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/101702851/journal.pone.0286681.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Digital Play","IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design","IT University of Copenhagen, Play, Culture, and AI, Games Research Group"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"gb","key":"the honourable society of lincoln s inn","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"The Honourable Society of Lincoln's Inn","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"au","key":"cquniversity","loc":["Australia","Oceania","OECD"],"name":"CQUniversity","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"gb","key":"university of bristol","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Bristol","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Leon Y.","key":"xiaopau","last":"Xiao","locid":"0a2a2206-180b-4443-bdb7-92deba889221","name":"Xiao, Leon Y.","orcid":"0000-0003-0709-0777","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Laura L.","key":"hendersonpau","last":"Henderson","name":"Henderson, Laura L.","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3 4","corresponding":null,"first":"Philip W.S.","key":"newallpau","last":"Newall","name":"Newall, Philip W.S.","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1371/journal.pone.0286681","issn":["19326203"],"issue":"9","journal":"PLOS ONE","paperid":"e0286681","publisher":"Public Library of Science","status":"p","title":"PLOS ONE","volume":"18"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85172420509"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-be07b61b-36da-449d-bad5-fa04f50b95c1"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"What are the odds? Poor compliance with UK loot box probability disclosure industry self-regulation","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-718911","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"The article synthesises what we learned from reviewing the player activism of the “Maple refugee” incident and applies the insights to the European video game industry and commercial context. The Maple Refugee incident was perhaps one of the most disruptive video game incidents that occurred in South Korea in recent years. It saw tens of thousands of Korean players from the game Maple Story (Nexon, 2003) mobilised in unprecedented online and offline protests in Spring 2021. Together with players from other free-to-play (F2P) games, Maple Story players rallied against the industry norms of monetising with loot boxes and the industry self-regulatory approach to probability disclosures to address potential harms. This culminated in the social phenomenon of the proxy activism method of ’truck protests,’ rallies of crowdfunded rented trucks displaying protest messages instead of people mass-gathering in public during the COVID-19 pandemic. Based on the English timeline of the incident collated by Park et al. (2023), we dive deeper into the case with a multidisciplinary group of experts from game studies, law, and human-computer interaction and highlight various issues present in this case: the regulation of loot boxes and probability disclosures, the social pillars of player activism, player trust and theorycrafting, and game production. The paper contributes to the deepening of the industry’s understanding of F2P game business while diversifying the Western-centric discourse of the game research landscape by calling for further cross-cultural and cross-disciplinary inquiries into current video game issues.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Park, Solip","Denoo, Maarten","Grosemans, Eva","Petrovskaya, Elena","Jin, Yaewon","Xiao, Leon Y."],"cuid":["S01-000.00-718931"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Asia, East","Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD"],"country":["Belgium","Finland","South Korea","United Kingdom"],"glorg":["Aalto University","All Global Affiliations","KU Leuven","University of Jyväskylä","University of York","Yonsei University"],"keyword":["Free to Play Games","Loot Boxes Regulation","Maple Refugee Incident","Player Activism","Probability Disclosures"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"learnings from the case of maple refugees a story of loot boxes probability disclosures and gamer consumer activism","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3616961.3616963","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-718931","identifier":[{"id":"85180007650","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-ed51f887-3fa9-43fa-89cf-65401a67f54c"],"keyword":["Free-to-Play Games","Loot Boxes Regulation","Maple Refugee Incident","Player Activism","Probability Disclosures"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-FF3FA810564DD2D0B31C7539BF806C7C"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3616961.3616963","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/102009511/3616961.3616963.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"fi","key":"aalto university","loc":["Finland","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Aalto University","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"be","key":"ku leuven","loc":["Belgium","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"KU Leuven","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"gb","key":"university of york","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of York","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"fi","key":"university of jyväskylä","loc":["Finland","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"University of Jyväskylä","used":1},{"affno":"5","country":"kr","key":"yonsei university","loc":["South Korea","Asia, East","OECD"],"name":"Yonsei University","used":1},{"affno":"6","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Digital Play","IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design","IT University of Copenhagen, Play, Culture, and AI, Games Research Group"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Solip","key":"parkpau","last":"Park","name":"Park, Solip","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Maarten","key":"denoopau","last":"Denoo","name":"Denoo, Maarten","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Eva","key":"grosemanspau","last":"Grosemans","name":"Grosemans, Eva","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Elena","key":"petrovskayapau","last":"Petrovskaya","name":"Petrovskaya, Elena","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4 5","corresponding":null,"first":"Yaewon","key":"jinpau","last":"Jin","name":"Jin, Yaewon","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"6","corresponding":null,"first":"Leon Y.","key":"xiaopau","last":"Xiao","locid":"0a2a2206-180b-4443-bdb7-92deba889221","name":"Xiao, Leon Y.","orcid":"0000-0003-0709-0777","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3616961.3616963","pages":"267","publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the 26th International Academic Mindtrek Conference (Mindtrek '23)"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85180007650"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-ed51f887-3fa9-43fa-89cf-65401a67f54c"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Learnings From The Case of Maple Refugees: A Story of Loot Boxes, Probability Disclosures, and Gamer Consumer Activism","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-718931","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"Blind boxes are novel, gambling-like physical products that consumers can buy to obtain randomized content. In January 2022, non-binding, advisory compliance guidelines recommending certain best practices when marketing blind boxes for sale were published in Shanghai, China. The relevant provisions, which include consumer protection and harm minimization measures such as setting age limits; publishing probability disclosures; and implementing “pity mechanics” that guarantee that the consumer will receive the rarer content that they desire after making a predetermined number of purchases, are translated and commented upon. Blind boxes and other gambling-like products are identified to also be available for sale in other countries, including in the West, where they may legally constitute “gambling” under existing laws but remain unregulated and available for purchase by children. The similarities and differences between blind boxes and video game loot boxes are highlighted to show that a stronger case exists (in law) to regulate physical gambling-like products than video game loot boxes generally: secondary markets demonstrably exist for the randomized physical blind box content, which as a result possesses real-world monetary value, whilst, in contrast, most virtual video game loot box content cannot be transferred to other players and therefore cannot be exchanged for real-world money. The emerging popularity of gambling-like products and the increasing “gamblification” of the provision of products and services generally causes issues that require gambling law reform to address: enforcing existing gambling law that did not contemplate such widespread gamblification indiscriminately to regulate everything may be overregulation and may overburden gambling regulators. However, gambling regulators that have made, and continue to maintain, a conscious executive decision not to enforce the law against obvious contraventions are failing to address potential consumer harms and arguably acting ultra vires: regulators must therefore publicly justify their decision, so that “gambling-like products” may be properly scrutinized.","author_count":1,"contributors":["Xiao, Leon Y."],"cuid":["S01-000.00-382748"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["consumer protection mainland China","cryptocurrencies and NFTs","gambling like products and practices","loot box regulation","protection of minors","video gaming law"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"blind boxes opening our eyes to the challenging regulation of gambling like products and gamblification and unexplained regulatory inaction","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1089/glr2.2022.0012","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-382748","ids":["itu-62952a12-a6a7-4522-ba08-e3f761abf82b"],"keyword":["consumer protection, mainland China","cryptocurrencies and NFTs","gambling-like products and practices","loot box regulation","protection of minors","video gaming law"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-3-3-F3167A4F406F5B598B9F8160F437224A"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1089/glr2.2022.0012","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/102674397/Preprint_-_Shanghai_Blind_Box_Regulation.pdf","version":"Pre-print"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"rem","url":"https://osf.io/c6epr/download","version":"Pre-print"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/102674399/xiao-2022-blind-boxes-opening-our-eyes-to-the-challenging-regulation-of-gambling-like-products-and-gamblification-and.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.31219/osf.io/c6epr","version":"Authors Final Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"rem","url":"https://www.liebertpub.com/doi/epdf/10.1089/glr2.2022.0012","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2022","yearSub":"2022"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Digital Play","IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design","IT University of Copenhagen, Play, Culture, and AI, Games Research Group"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Leon Y.","key":"xiaopau","last":"Xiao","locid":"0a2a2206-180b-4443-bdb7-92deba889221","name":"Xiao, Leon Y.","orcid":"0000-0003-0709-0777","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.31219/osf.io/c6epr","issn":["25725300"],"issue":"5","journal":"Gaming Law Review","pages":"255-268","publisher":"Mary Ann Liebert Inc.","publisher_org":"Mary Ann Liebert, Inc.","status":"p","title":"Gaming Law Review","volume":"26"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-62952a12-a6a7-4522-ba08-e3f761abf82b"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Blind Boxes: Opening Our Eyes to the Challenging Regulation of Gambling-Like Products and Gamblification and Unexplained Regulatory Inaction","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-382748","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"Paid loot boxes are video game monetisation methods that provide randomised rewards of varying value. Loot boxes are prevalent internationally: approximately 60% of the highest-grossing mobile games in ‘Western’ countries contain loot boxes (Zendle et al., 2020a), as do approximately 90% in the People’s Republic of China (PRC) (Xiao et al., 2021).1 Loot boxes represent an important revenue stream for the industry: the sale of loot boxes in one single game can generate more than US$528,000 per day from just one country alone (Zendle et al., 2020b). Global loot box spending was estimated to have been US$15 billion in 2020, and is estimated to rise and exceed US$20 billion by 2025 (Juniper Research et al., 2021).","author_count":3,"contributors":["Xiao, Leon Y.","Henderson, Laura L.","Newall, Philip W.S."],"cuid":["S01-000.00-701232"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","OECD","Oceania"],"country":["Australia","United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","CQUniversity","The Honourable Society of Lincoln's Inn"],"keyword":["Loot boxes","Mobile games","Randomised rewards","Revenue stream","Video game monetisation"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"what are the odds lower compliance with western loot box probability disclosure industry self regulation than chinese legal regulation","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dca","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0286681","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-701232","identifier":[{"id":"85172420509","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-3d58fb1e-7050-4a70-8c62-51a2b0130dbd"],"keyword":["Loot boxes","Mobile games","Randomised rewards","Revenue stream","Video game monetisation"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-39D819A9F19AAF8B52F74E6B6973A09D"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"rem","url":"http://www.digra.org/digital-library/publications/what-are-the-odds-lower-compliance-with-western-loot-box-probability-disclosure-industry-selfregulation-than-chinese-legal-regulation/","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/101904361/DiGRA_2022_paper_13.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0286681","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Digital Play","IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design","IT University of Copenhagen, Play, Culture, and AI, Games Research Group"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"gb","key":"the honourable society of lincoln s inn","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"The Honourable Society of Lincoln's Inn","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"au","key":"cquniversity","loc":["Australia","Oceania","OECD"],"name":"CQUniversity","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Leon Y.","key":"xiaopau","last":"Xiao","locid":"0a2a2206-180b-4443-bdb7-92deba889221","name":"Xiao, Leon Y.","orcid":"0000-0003-0709-0777","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Laura L.","key":"hendersonpau","last":"Henderson","name":"Henderson, Laura L.","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Philip W.S.","key":"newallpau","last":"Newall","name":"Newall, Philip W.S.","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1371/journal.pone.0286681","publisher":"Digital Games Research Association","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the Digital Games Research Association (DiGRA) International Conference 2022: Bringing Worlds Together"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-3d58fb1e-7050-4a70-8c62-51a2b0130dbd"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"What are the odds? Lower compliance with Western loot box probability disclosure industry self- regulation than Chinese legal regulation","type":"Conference Abstract","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-701232","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"Loot boxes are virtual items in video games that players purchase to obtain randomised rewards of varying value. Such randomised monetisation methods are prevalently implemented globally. Loot boxes are conceptually and structurally akin to gambling, and their purchase is positively correlated with problem gambling in Western countries. Given the potential harms loot boxes may cause, particularly to vulnerable consumers, e.g. children, regulators and policymakers are paying increasing attention. Some countries, e.g. Belgium, have actively enforced existing gambling laws to ban certain loot box implementations. However, less restrictive regulatory approaches, e.g. requiring probability disclosures, are also being considered. Amendments to existing law and new laws dedicated to regulating loot boxes are likely forthcoming in many countries. Companies’ discretionary and suboptimal compliance with loot box probability disclosure law in the People’s Republic of China reveals how future loot box laws and industry self-regulations should be better drafted to ensure maximum consumer protection.","author_count":1,"contributors":["Xiao, Leon Y."],"cuid":["S01-000.00-383182"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Consumer protection law","Gambling","Loot boxes","Mainland China","Video game regulation","Video gaming"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"drafting video game loot box regulation for dummies a chinese lesson","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1080/13600834.2022.2088062","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-383182","identifier":[{"id":"85132104979","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-823f91e8-758e-445a-bc09-3946145cfe5e"],"keyword":["Gambling","Mainland China","consumer protection law","loot boxes","video game regulation","video gaming"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-3-5-C3ECBB1F533810C4039B84AD5470B954"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.31219/osf.io/b6x97","version":"Authors Final Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1080/13600834.2022.2088062","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/86816783/Xiao_2022_Drafting_video_game_loot_box_regulation_for_dummies_a_Chinese_lesson.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Digital Play","IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design","IT University of Copenhagen, Play, Culture, and AI, Games Research Group"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Leon Y.","key":"xiaopau","last":"Xiao","locid":"0a2a2206-180b-4443-bdb7-92deba889221","name":"Xiao, Leon Y.","orcid":"0000-0003-0709-0777","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.31219/osf.io/b6x97","issn":["13600834","14698404"],"issue":"3","journal":"Information and Communications Technology Law","publisher":"Taylor & Francis","status":"p","title":"Information and Communications Technology Law","volume":"31"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85132104979"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-823f91e8-758e-445a-bc09-3946145cfe5e"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Drafting Video Game Loot Box Regulation for Dummies: A Chinese Lesson","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-383182","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"author_count":1,"contributors":["Xiao, Leon Y."],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384345"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"level":"pop","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"und","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"loot boxes are not gambling under dutch law","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dna","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://www.scl.org/news/12540-loot-boxes-are-not-gambling-under-dutch-law","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-384345","ids":["itu-d1bdabd8-7865-4307-ba4a-ab18d0f50704"],"lang":"English","level":"Popular","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-215F6E8F1578BC3543F4722F14E18F78"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://www.scl.org/news/12540-loot-boxes-are-not-gambling-under-dutch-law","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Digital Play","IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design","IT University of Copenhagen, Play, Culture, and AI, Games Research Group"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Leon Y.","key":"xiaopau","last":"Xiao","locid":"0a2a2206-180b-4443-bdb7-92deba889221","name":"Xiao, Leon Y.","orcid":"0000-0003-0709-0777","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"issn":["01403249","27562417"],"newspaper":"Computers & Law","pages":"24-25","status":"p","title":"Computers & Law","volume":"145"},"review":"Undetermined","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-d1bdabd8-7865-4307-ba4a-ab18d0f50704"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Loot boxes are NOT gambling under Dutch law","type":"Newspaper Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384345","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"\"Loot boxes\" are video game mechanics that provide randomized rewards. \"Paid loot boxes\" that require purchase using real-world money by players are frequently implemented by companies to \"monetize\" and generate revenue. The technical definition of \"loot boxes\" is wider than that of when the term is used in common parlance, which generally only refers to \"paid loot boxes.\" Previous research has identified conceptual and psychological similarities between paid loot boxes and gambling. Regulation and other harm-minimization measures have been suggested and adopted in certain countries. Further research is required to understand the nature and the extent of any potential harms.","author_count":1,"contributors":["Xiao, Leon Y."],"cuid":["S01-000.00-381696"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Gambling similarities","Loot boxes","Paid loot boxes","Regulation and harm minimization","Video game monetization"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"nr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"loot boxes","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dw","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/87082376/Final_Print_Out.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-381696","ids":["itu-1cc3a810-b7f9-4f5b-a7f6-9842d21664f5"],"keyword":["Gambling similarities","Loot boxes","Paid loot boxes","Regulation and harm minimization","Video game monetization"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-2D7860E1846FBFF06CDE8E750FF6E812"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://eolt.org/articles/loot-boxes","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/87082376/Final_Print_Out.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Digital Play","IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design","IT University of Copenhagen, Play, Culture, and AI, Games Research Group"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Leon Y.","key":"xiaopau","last":"Xiao","locid":"0a2a2206-180b-4443-bdb7-92deba889221","name":"Xiao, Leon Y.","orcid":"0000-0003-0709-0777","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"place":"Copenhagen","publisher":"ERC-Advanced-Grant project MSG - Making Sense of Games (2016 - 2022, PI Espen Aarseth)","status":"p"},"review":"No Review","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-1cc3a810-b7f9-4f5b-a7f6-9842d21664f5"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Loot Boxes","type":"Working Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-381696","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"Leon Y. Xiao ponders what 2022 might bring for video games regulation and how states might curb the growing trend for loot boxes","author_count":1,"contributors":["Xiao, Leon Y."],"cuid":["S01-000.00-381832"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Queen Mary University of London"],"keyword":["Consumer protection","Digital gaming trends","Gambling legislation","Loot boxes","Video game regulation"],"level":"pop","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"und","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"what next for video game regulation in 000000002022","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dna","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"rem","url":"https://www.scl.org/news/12485-what-next-for-video-game-regulation-in-2022","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-381832","ids":["itu-2563bbdc-d5d4-4d91-8001-eb96f5561851"],"keyword":["Consumer protection","Digital gaming trends","Gambling legislation","Loot boxes","Video game regulation"],"lang":"English","level":"Popular","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-DAAC7DB0BEB017F3297F7E96F8DCF634"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"rem","url":"https://www.scl.org/news/12485-what-next-for-video-game-regulation-in-2022","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design","IT University of Copenhagen, Play, Culture, and AI, Games Research Group"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"gb","key":"queen mary university of london","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"Queen Mary University of London","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"first":"Leon Y.","key":"xiaopau","last":"Xiao","locid":"0a2a2206-180b-4443-bdb7-92deba889221","name":"Xiao, Leon Y.","orcid":"0000-0003-0709-0777","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"issn":["01403249","27562417"],"issn_org":["01403249"],"newspaper":"Computers & Law","pages":"39-40","status":"p","title":"Computers & Law","volume":"144"},"review":"Undetermined","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-2563bbdc-d5d4-4d91-8001-eb96f5561851"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"What next for video game regulation in 2022?","type":"Newspaper Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-381832","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"Objective: Online gambling has increased the accessibility and range of gambling products available to people all over the world. This trend has been particularly noticeable in the United Kingdom. Cryptocurrency-based gambling is a new, largely unregulated, way to gamble online, which uses mostly anonymous blockchain-based technologies, such as Bitcoin. The present research investigated consumer protection features of 40 frequently visited and U.K.-accessible cryptocurrency-based online gambling operators. Method: A content analysis was performed by visiting all 40 cryptocurrency-based online operators and recording their safer gambling and consumer protection practices. Coded features included aspects of the sign-up process, features of any safer gambling pages, customer support practices, and Identity verification. Results: Results revealed significant failings in the account registration process; none of the operators verified the identity of new users, and 35% required only an email or no personal information for sign-up. Overall, 37.5% of operators offered no safer gambling tools and a further 20% offered only one. Additionally, 64.7% of operators continued to email promotional material after being informed of a user’s impaired control when gambling. Less than half of the analyzed operators held a valid license (47.5%), and none of the operators with an available deposit page required identity verification before enabling deposits. Conclusions: These results highlight the potential risks for young and vulnerable individuals, especially when a lack of identity verification is paired with the inherent anonymity of cryptocurrencies. Furthermore, it emphasizes the need for greater policy and research attention toward cryptocurrency-based online gambling.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Andrade, Maira","Sharman, Steve","Xiao, Leon Y.","Newall, Philip W.S."],"cuid":["S01-000.00-382430"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","OECD","Oceania"],"country":["Australia","United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","CQUniversity","King’s College London","University of Bristol","University of East London"],"keyword":["Regulation","Responsible gambling","crypto gambling","online betting","safer gambling"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"safer gambling and consumer protection failings among 000000000040 frequently visited cryptocurrency based online gambling operators","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1037/adb0000885","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-382430","identifier":[{"id":"85147564821","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-4dc80ea1-7999-4cc8-8d69-84a767dba719"],"keyword":["crypto gambling","online betting","regulation","responsible gambling","safer gambling"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-463340D89B41D447EE1F2B4B585C75A7"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1037/adb0000885","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/90412847/2023_16166_001.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2022","yearSub":"2022"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"gb","key":"university of east london","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of East London","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"gb","key":"king s college london","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"King’s College London","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design","IT University of Copenhagen, Play, Culture, and AI, Games Research Group"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"au","key":"cquniversity","loc":["Australia","Oceania","OECD"],"name":"CQUniversity","used":1},{"affno":"5","country":"gb","key":"university of bristol","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Bristol","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Maira","key":"andradepau","last":"Andrade","name":"Andrade, Maira","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Steve","key":"sharmanpau","last":"Sharman","name":"Sharman, Steve","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Leon Y.","key":"xiaopau","last":"Xiao","locid":"0a2a2206-180b-4443-bdb7-92deba889221","name":"Xiao, Leon Y.","orcid":"0000-0003-0709-0777","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4 5","corresponding":null,"first":"Philip W.S.","key":"newallpau","last":"Newall","name":"Newall, Philip W.S.","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1037/adb0000885","issn":["0893164X","19391501"],"issn_org":["0893164X"],"journal":"Psychology of Addictive Behaviors","publisher":"American Psychological Association","status":"p","title":"Psychology of Addictive Behaviors"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85147564821"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-4dc80ea1-7999-4cc8-8d69-84a767dba719"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Safer Gambling and Consumer Protection Failings Among 40 Frequently Visited Cryptocurrency-Based Online Gambling Operators","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-382430","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"author_count":1,"contributors":["Xiao, Leon Y."],"cuid":["S01-000.00-382662"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"towards reproducibility in game analysis some reflections on the study design and methodology of loot box prevalence studies","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dca","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/86816757/Xiao_in_GAP_Proceedings.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-382662","ids":["itu-5cf405ba-10d8-4ac0-a72a-b2f1ea81103f"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-C616B4CAE462D0E67B02CE5DD0798C8A"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/86816757/Xiao_in_GAP_Proceedings.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://blogit.itu.dk/msgproject/wp-content/uploads/sites/35/2022/04/conference-proceedings.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Digital Play","IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design","IT University of Copenhagen, Play, Culture, and AI, Games Research Group"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Leon Y.","key":"xiaopau","last":"Xiao","locid":"0a2a2206-180b-4443-bdb7-92deba889221","name":"Xiao, Leon Y.","orcid":"0000-0003-0709-0777","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"pages":"34-39","place":"Copenhagen","publisher":"IT University of Copenhagen","status":"p","subtitle":"Game Analysis Perspective Conference","title":"Conference Proceedings"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-5cf405ba-10d8-4ac0-a72a-b2f1ea81103f"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Towards Reproducibility in Game Analysis: Some Reflections on the Study Design and Methodology of Loot Box Prevalence Studies","type":"Conference Abstract","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-382662","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"Loot boxes are quasi-gambling virtual products in video games that provide randomised rewards of varying value. Previous studies in Western contexts have identified a positive correlation between loot box purchasing and problem gambling severity. A preregistered survey of People’s Republic of China (PRC) video game players (N = 879) failed to replicate this correlation. We observed statistically significant but weak positive correlations between loot box expenditure and past-year gambling participation, and between loot box expenditure and impulsiveness. This study cannot prove that loot boxes are not disproportionately purchased by people with problem gambling symptomatology in the PRC or that PRC players are not potentially at risk of loot box-related harms. Instead, the evidence suggests that the relationship between loot boxes and gambling might be weaker in the PRC than in Western countries. We identified multiple unique factors about the PRC that might be affecting this relationship. For example, the lotteries are the only legally permitted form of gambling. More gamified electronic gambling products are unavailable. The limited availability of gambling meant that a low level of gambling participation (n = 87) was observed, which is a limitation of this study. Additionally, the PRC is presently the only country to legally require loot box probability disclosures as a consumer protection measure. Most loot box purchasers (84.6%) reported seeing loot box probability disclosures, but only 19.3% of this group reported consequently spending less money. Most loot box purchasers (86.9%) thought that pity-timers, which increase the winning probabilities of obtaining rarer rewards, are appropriate for implementation. Future loot box research should give greater consideration to differing cultural contexts and novel consumer protection measures.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Xiao, Leon Y.","Fraser, Tullia C.","Newall, Philip W.S."],"cuid":["S01-000.00-382286"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","OECD","Oceania"],"country":["Australia","United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","CQUniversity","University of Bristol"],"keyword":["Consumer protection law","Gambling","Loot boxes","Mainland China","Video game regulations","Video gaming"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"opening pandora s loot box weak links between gambling and loot box expenditure in china and player opinions on probability disclosures and pity timers","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s10899-022-10148-0","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-382286","identifier":[{"id":"85135575170","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-44bb2a88-5b64-4b4f-99a9-73ca6927dbec"],"keyword":["Consumer protection law","Gambling","Loot boxes","Mainland China","Video game regulations","Video gaming"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-0FA684FBAD8C077AC084485310D779A1"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/86941147/s10899_022_10148_0.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s10899-022-10148-0","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Digital Play","IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design","IT University of Copenhagen, Play, Culture, and AI, Games Research Group"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"au","key":"cquniversity","loc":["Australia","Oceania","OECD"],"name":"CQUniversity","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"gb","key":"university of bristol","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Bristol","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Leon Y.","key":"xiaopau","last":"Xiao","locid":"0a2a2206-180b-4443-bdb7-92deba889221","name":"Xiao, Leon Y.","orcid":"0000-0003-0709-0777","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Tullia C.","key":"fraserpau","last":"Fraser","name":"Fraser, Tullia C.","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 3","corresponding":null,"first":"Philip W.S.","key":"newallpau","last":"Newall","name":"Newall, Philip W.S.","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/s10899-022-10148-0","issn":["15733602"],"journal":"Journal of Gambling Studies","publisher":"Springer","status":"p","title":"Journal of Gambling Studies"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85135575170"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-44bb2a88-5b64-4b4f-99a9-73ca6927dbec"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Opening Pandora’s Loot Box: Weak Links Between Gambling and Loot Box Expenditure in China, and Player Opinions on Probability Disclosures and Pity-Timers","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-382286","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"Purpose of Review Loot boxes are gambling-like monetisation mechanics in video games that are purchased for opportunities to obtain randomised in-game rewards. Gambling regulation is increasingly being informed by insights from public health. Despite conceptual similarities between loot boxes and gambling, there is much less international consensus on loot box regulation. Various approaches to regulating loot boxes are reviewed via a public health framework that highlights various trade-offs between individual liberties and harm prevention. Recent Findings Many countries have considered regulation, but as yet only a few countries have taken tangible actions. Existing regulatory approaches vary greatly. More restrictively, Belgium has effectively ‘banned’ paid loot boxes and prohibits their sale to both children and adults. In contrast, more liberally, China only requires disclosure of the probabilities of obtaining potential rewards to provide transparency and perhaps help players to make more informed purchasing decisions. Most other countries (e.g., the UK) have adopted a ‘wait-and-watch’ approach by neither regulating loot box sales nor providing any dedicated consumer protection response. Industry self-regulation has also been adopted, although this appears to elicit lower rates of compliance than comparable national legal regulation. Summary Many potential public health approaches to loot box regulation, such as expenditure limits or harm-reducing modifications to loot box design (e.g., fairer reward structures), deserve further attention. The compliance and clinical benefits of existing interventions (including varying degrees of regulation, as adopted by different countries, and industry self-regulation) should be further assessed. The current international variation in loot box regulation presents opportunities to compare the merits of different approaches over time.","author_count":1,"contributors":["Xiao, Leon Y."],"cuid":["S01-000.00-382318"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Consumer protection","Interactive entertainment law","Loot boxes","Online gambling","Public health","Video games","Video gaming regulation"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"regulating gambling like video game loot boxes a public health framework comparing industry self regulation existing national legal approaches and other potential approaches","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s40429-022-00424-9","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-382318","identifier":[{"id":"85134793518","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-467fab83-bc26-4021-95e6-add48edb1e8f"],"keyword":["Consumer protection","Interactive entertainment law","Loot boxes","Online gambling","Public health","Video games","Video gaming regulation"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-56A4F0B23D16A8F2892AAEDEAE88CDAF"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/86907341/s40429_022_00424_9.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s40429-022-00424-9","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design","IT University of Copenhagen, Play, Culture, and AI, Games Research Group"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Leon Y.","key":"xiaopau","last":"Xiao","locid":"0a2a2206-180b-4443-bdb7-92deba889221","name":"Xiao, Leon Y.","orcid":"0000-0003-0709-0777","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/s40429-022-00424-9","issn":["21962952"],"journal":"Current Addiction Reports","publisher":"Springer","status":"p","title":"Current Addiction Reports"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85134793518"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-467fab83-bc26-4021-95e6-add48edb1e8f"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Regulating Gambling-Like Video Game Loot Boxes: a Public Health Framework Comparing Industry Self-Regulation, Existing National Legal Approaches, and Other Potential Approaches","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-382318","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"Loot boxes are virtual products in video games that provide randomized rewards, and accordingly share structural similarities with gambling. Policymakers around the world are presently considering how best to regulate loot boxes. Current loot box consumer protection measures, such as requiring probability disclosures, have been inspired by similar harm reduction approaches in gambling. However, unlike in many gambling games, most loot boxes’ reward structures are arguably too complex for consumers to be meaningfully protected by probability disclosures alone. But promisingly, loot boxes can readily be redesigned to be more ethical because they are digital products. Based on behavioural science principles, this article proposes four reductions to loot box reward complexity. The decisionmaking environment can be simplified by capping (i) the maximum number of loot boxes per game and (ii) the maximum number of potential rewards per loot box, and by (iii) equalizing winning probabilities across rewards. Additionally, (iv) companies can implement “exhaustible” loot boxes that provide the player with every potential reward after a predetermined amount of money has been spent, thereby effectively instituting a maximum spending limit. These ethical game design proposals can credibly reduce financial harms from loot boxes while both maintaining consumer freedom and preserving companies’ commercial interests.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Xiao, Leon Y.","Newall, Philip W.S."],"cuid":["S01-000.00-381823"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","OECD","Oceania"],"country":["Australia","United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","CQUniversity","City, University of London","Queen Mary University of London","The Honourable Society of Lincoln's Inn","University of Bristol"],"keyword":["Consumer protection law","Ethical Game Design","Gambling","Gaming Law","Loot boxes","Microtransactions","Video Gaming Regulations","Video games"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"probability disclosures are not enough reducing loot box reward complexity as a part of ethical video game design","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/86751190/JGI_Jul_21_POL_022.R2_FINAL_Clean.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-381823","identifier":[{"id":"85172235430","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-250312b5-1eda-4f28-8c1c-2080f785e504"],"keyword":["Consumer Protection Law","Ethical Game Design","Gambling","Gaming Law","Loot Boxes","Microtransactions","Video Games","Video Gaming Regulations"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-3-4-52845D8DC5E55E673CAFA1250D9B3D59"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"rem","url":"https://web.archive.org/web/20220517082108/https://cdspress.ca/?p=5602","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/86751190/JGI_Jul_21_POL_022.R2_FINAL_Clean.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.31234/osf.io/nuksd","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2022","yearSub":"2022"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Digital Play","IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design","IT University of 